                          BBS Split 1.10
                          --------------

        Welcome to BBS Split. BBS Split's main purpose is to
        compare two BBS indexes for duplicate files and write
        the output to a file.


        Requirements:
        -------------

        Amiga (BBSSplit) :  AmigaOS 1.3 or newer, 68000 or better.
        DOS Real Mode (BBSSplit.exe) :  DOS, 80386 or better.
        DOS Protected Mode (BBSSpPro.exe) : DOS, DPMI-server, 
                                            80386 or better.

        Usage:
        ------

        BBSSplit <INDEXFILE1> <INDEXFILE2> <OUTPUTFILE>
        INDEXFILE1 is compared to INDEXFILE2 and the output
        written to OUTPUTFILE.

        BBS Split uses configuration file (BBSSplit.ini). It's up
        to you what you want to include in here but don't mess with
        lines with square brackets[], execpt those you make yourself.
        [Don't compare these strings!] line should always be the first.

        After [Don't compare these strings!] line you can place words
        that should not be checked, eg. those that are not file names
        at all. You should only be concerned about words that start at
        the same column as file names and are in the Index file 1.
        Strings can be disabled by placing space(s) or tabulator(s)
        before them.

        After [Area headlines] line you can place first words of the
        area headlines used in Index files eg. Area, Area: and [Packers].
        Strings can be disabled by placing space(s) or tabulator(s)
        before them.

        After [Wanted areas] line you can place one word from each
        area's area headline you want to search. You should choose
        words that do not appear in any other area headline, unless
        you want to set several areas with one word. The word doesn't
        have to be the first word in the area headline. [Wanted areas]
        only checks index file 1. Strings can be disabled by placing 
        space(s) or tabulator(s) before them. All areas are processed
        if you place no strings after [Wanted Areas]

        [NOWLW2 -  1: WINDOWS Business / Finance]
        [NOWLW2 -  4: WINDOWS Editors / Text Utils]

        From the above headlines I would choose 1: and 4: as the
        words. Finance] and Editors would also be quite good choises.
        Note that Finance has ] in the end because in the headline
        the word stops after ]. [Wanted areas] works for index file 1
        only. This option can be turned off by removing all the words.

        After [String stop] line you can place a separator character
        that stops the string reading. Placing . here will makes
        possible to identify for example BBSSplit.lha and BBSSplit.lzx
        as the same file. This option can be turned off by removing the
        separator character.

        After [Index 2 write] line you can place a number between 1 and 
        9999 or a or A if you want lines of duplicate entry in index file 
        2 to be written to output file after the complete description of
        the same file in index file 1.  Numbers 1 to 9999 mean the amount
        of lines to be written and a or A mean all lines. If more than one
        duplicate of the same file is found, all the duplicates are
        written after the complete description. Any other characters turn
        this option off.

        After [Write headlines] line you can place b or B (both indexes),
        1 (index file 1) or 2 (index file 2) if you want the area
        headlines to be written to the output file. Index file 2's
        area headlines are written in more compact way (only one line)
        than index file 1's (several lines).

        After [Identical not saved] line you can place y or Y if you want
        to compare two identical indexes for only duplicates that are not
        at the same line in index file 1 and 2. Any other character turns
        this option off.

        After [Case not sensitive] line you can place y or Y if you want
        to turn case sensitivity off. This makes possible to identify
        BBSSplit.lha and BBSSPLIT.LHA as a same file. [Case not sensitive]
        only affects to comparison of file names.

        After [Starting column] you can place the column (usually 1) for
        index file 1 and index file 2 where the file names and area
        headlines start. A tabulator is counted as a one column. Normally
        if a character in the starting column is a tabulator or a space,
        that line will be skipped. With Spaces are OK and Tabulators are
        OK you can disable this feature. This can be done for both
        (b or B) index files, for only index file 1 (1) or for only index
        file 2 (2). Any other characters turn these options off.

        [Date matching] can be activated by several characters.

        If you want only files whose dates are the same in index file
        1 and index file 2, use s or S. If you want only files whose dates
        are not the same in index file 1 and index file 2, use d or D.

        If you want only files whose date is between certain range
        use b, B, 1 or 2. b or B mean both index files. 1 means
        index file 1 only and 2 means index file 2 only. Dates at the
        end of the BBSSplit.ini are used in these cases.

        Any other character than those above turns [Date matching] off.

        Starting column is the column where the date starts. Separator
        character is a character which separates the fields of the date
        from each other. \ should not be used as a separator character.
        Use the same separator character is used for BBSSplit.ini and
        index file 1.

        Use the same date format in BBSSplit.ini and index file 1.

        Supported date formats:

        A or a  = 30.07.74 or 30.07.1974
        B or b  = 07.30.74 or 07.30.1974
        C or c  = 74.30.07 or 1974.30.07
        D or d  = 74.07.30 or 1974.07.30

        Beginning zeroes are not necessary, so 30.7.1974 works as
        fine as 30.07.1974. Years lower than 40 will be converted
        to 21 century. For example year 23 will be converted to 2023,
        but year 1923 will remain the same.


        If you didn't understand what the heck I was talking about,
        just try to alter the settings and see what happens.


        How to make BBS Split run faster:
        ---------------------------------

        Only activate options, you need. Only define the needed
        words for [Don't compare these strings] and [Area headlines].
        If possible, use [String stop]. Define a small number for
        [Index 2 write]. Use [Wanted Areas] whenever possible.


        History:
        --------

        1.0  June 12, 1996

        Initial release.

        1.01 June 12, 1996

        Lines starting with a tabulator will be skipped in index file 1.

        1.02 June 16, 1996

        Added [Identical not saved].

        1.03 June 17, 1996

        Now progress indicator and number of lines counters show
        more than 65535 lines.

        1.04 June 24, 1996

        Improved [Index 2 write].
        Addes comments to BBSSplit.ini.
        Now more than one duplicate of the same file is found.
        Added [Case not sensitive].

        1.05 June 25, 1996

        Lines starting with a carriage return will be skipped in
        index file 1.
        Fixed a bug in progress counter.
        Added [Date matching].

        1.06 June 26, 1996

        Added [Starting column].
        Added [Wanted areas].
        Writes the area headline to the output file only if a
        duplicate was found on that area.

        1.07 July 3, 1996

        Fixed bugs in Spaces are OK and Tabulators are OK.
        Starting column of area headlines can now be defined.
        Fixed bugs in [Case not sensitive].
        Optimized code. BBS Split is now smaller and a little
        bit faster.

        1.08 July 4, 1996

        Optimized code.

        1.09 July 9, 1996

        Optimized code.
        Fixed [Identical not saved].
        Addes [Write headlines].
        Fixed [Date matching].

        1.10 August 8, 1996

        Optimized code.
        DOS Protected Mode version is now available. It is
        up to 40% faster than the Real Mode version.
        Maximum lengt of filenames in indexes is now 79
        characters. This might affect the appearance of the
        output file dramatically, so you might have to put more 
        strings in [Don't compare these strings]. Maximum length 
        of lines is still 81 characters. These will change in the 
        future as BBSSplit becomes more dynamic.


